KPL/MYB

The equinox-based conventions of the IAU (1976-1982): the precession of J. H. Lieske et al.
(1977), the mean obliquity and the 1980 nutation series (P. K. Seidelmann, Celest. Mech. 27,
79, 1982; the theory of J. M. Wahr, 1981), and the Greenwich mean sidereal time of S. Aoki et
al. (1982). Read by myb_equinox_create (myb/frames.h) with the prefix 'IAU1980'; the library
itself knows none of these numbers. These are the frames of the FK5 era and of the two-line
element sets (TEME); for current work use the CIO-based IAU 2006/2000A (iau2006-cip.tk).

Sources
  ERFA (BSD-3-Clause, derived from IAU SOFA), revision
  1a8044cde5b7763295d472a6443387239127c6c8: nut80.c (the 106 terms, SHA-256
  b8fe57d61aaa80f50db2e0522b99737c96aeab9405ee8de21e3d576385d5ebf5; the fundamental arguments), prec76.c,
  obl80.c, gmst82.c. Numbers are copied without change; the whole revolutions of the
  arguments (1325, 99, 1342, 1236 and -5 turns per century) are folded into their linear
  coefficients (1 turn = 1296000 arcseconds).

Form (myb_equinox_create)
  t = (TT - J2000.0) in Julian centuries of 36525 days (these conventions do not distinguish
  TT from TDB).
  Fundamental arguments l, l', F, D, Omega: F_k(t) = sum_i POLYNOMIAL[5 (k-1) + i] t^i
  arcseconds, reduced modulo 1296000.
  Nutation in longitude and obliquity (units of 0.1 milliarcsecond):
    DPSI = sum (A + A' t) sin ARG,   DEPS = sum (B + B' t) cos ARG,   ARG = sum_k n_k F_k,
  each term being 3 + 5 numbers: j (power of t), a_s, a_c, n_l n_l' n_F n_D n_Omega.
  Terms of zero amplitude are omitted.
  Mean obliquity of the ecliptic: eps_A = sum_i OBLIQUITY[i] t^i arcseconds.
  Precession from J2000.0 (Lieske et al. 1977 with T0 = 0): zeta_A, z_A, theta_A =
  sum_i PRECESSION_*[i] t^i arcseconds; the rotation from the mean equator and equinox of
  J2000.0 to those of date is R3(-z_A) R2(theta_A) R3(-zeta_A).
  Greenwich mean sidereal time (Aoki et al. 1982): GMST = 2 pi / 86400 (GMST[0] + GMST[1] Tu
  + GMST[2] Tu^2 + GMST[3] Tu^3 + seconds of UT1 since 0h) radians, Tu = (UT1 - J2000.0) in
  Julian centuries; GMST[0] is the value at 0h UT1 of 2000 January 1.

